First Author: Hsich
Title: Heart Transplantation: An In-Depth Survival Analysis
Source number: 20  ￼

Very short summary: This national SRTR study examined post-heart-transplant survival using both temporal decomposition and Random Survival Forests. Five key findings were: (1) 30,606 adult isolated heart-transplant recipients were included; (2) 8,278 deaths occurred during a median 3.9-year follow-up; (3) three mortality phases were identified—early, constant, and late; (4) sex was not an important overall mortality risk factor, although a few sex-specific interactions were found; and (5) key predictors included recipient and donor age, bilirubin, renal function, BMI, congenital heart disease, mechanical ventilation, ECMO, diabetes, ischemic time, and donor-recipient interactions. Tables 1–3, Figure 1, the Central Illustration, and the interaction figures described in the Results were used for this extraction.

